"The word Kâria is derived from the root 'kar'', meaning to strike violently. In the lexicon, it carries meanings such as a mallet, knocking on a door, and clashing with swords. Over time, it became a name for great calamities and especially for the Resurrection. In the Quran, in the form 'el-Kâria', it is a proper name expressing the terror of the Resurrection.\n\nSeveral reasons have been put forward as to why Kâria was given this name: First, that it is the initial terrifying cry (the blowing of the Trumpet [Sur]) that will bewilder people's minds. Second, the violent collision of celestial and terrestrial bodies during the destruction of the cosmos. Third, that it strikes people with terror. Fourth, that it strikes the enemies of the truth with punishment and torment. This last view of Muqatil has been considered stronger.\n\nThe expression 'What is that kâria?' in the verses is not an actual question, but is intended to instill fear. The repetition of the name 'kâria' three times reinforces this intimidation. According to some scholars, this expression is a warning in the sense of 'beware, there is the kâria!' The true nature of kâria cannot be fully known by anyone until it is actually witnessed.\n\nOn the Day of Resurrection, people are likened to scattered and fluttering moths (butterflies). This simile depicts the bewilderment and wretchedness arising from the terror of that day. Although it has also been stated that they are likened to locusts, the simile of moths is more widespread.\n\nThe mountains, on the other hand, will become like carded colored wool. First they crumble, then they turn into a heap of sand, and then, dispersing like clouds, they turn into carded wool. This resembles a volcanic eruption of the earth. This first stage of the Resurrection encompasses the period between death and resurrection.\n\nThose whose scales are heavy—that is, those whose good deeds outweigh their sins—will be in a pleasing life. The word 'mevâzîn' (scales) may be the plural of 'mîzân', just as it may be the plural of 'mevzûn' (the deeds weighed). The weighing of deeds is a reality, but there are different views regarding how it will be carried out: According to some, the record books of deeds are weighed, while according to others, the weighing is done by good and bad deeds taking physical form. It is obligatory (wajib) to believe in the reality of the scale, but knowing its exact manner is not a condition.\n\nThe destination of those whose scales are light is 'hâviye' (the abyss). 'Hâviye' is one of the names of Hell and denotes a deep abyss. The expression 'His mother is hâviye' has been interpreted in different ways: as being a refuge like a mother's embrace, as an expression of a curse, or as meaning the crown of his head. Ultimately, this conveys being cast into the bosom of Hell or falling headlong.\n\nAfter asking what hâviye is, it is answered: 'It is a blazing fire.' This fire is fierce and scorching, forming the lowest level of Hell. This very fire is the eternal abode of those whose scales are light."